# Mutation cases for §scxml-B-2-8-1 — a payload the datamodel could not read
# arrives as a space-normalized string, and the host that built it can find out.
#
# Run with `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/undecodable_payload_is_reported.cases`.
#
# The clause gives a payload three readings and names the third "otherwise".
# Delivering the string is the clause; staying silent about WHY it is a string
# is not part of it. A host that serialized `{"done":true}` and got `{"done":`
# past a truncation sees the document evaluate `_event.data.done`, find nothing,
# and take exactly the transition it would have taken had the field been
# legitimately absent — same state, same configuration, same datamodel counters.
#
# The trap these cases exist for is the third one below. The reading is decided
# inside the script engine, which is the only place that knows whether a
# structured parse was ATTEMPTED, and the value it hands back is a value the
# call site can drop with no compiler complaining — which is what every backend
# did with it before this axis (`let _ =`, `.get();`, `_ =`). A mutation that
# reinstates the drop leaves every other assertion in the suite passing.
#
# The C++ engines and the C11 template halves of the same axis live in
# `undecodable_payload_is_reported_ctest.cases`.
#
# Anchors are literal slices of the source; an edit that moves one is reported
# INCONCLUSIVE with the anchor quoted, never as a pass.

mutation_tests -p sce-rust-tests --test undecodable_payload_is_reported
mutation_targets backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs
mutation_targets backends/rust/lua/src/lib.rs
mutation_targets backends/rust/runtime/src/scripting/i_script_engine.rs
# The third-rung classifier lives in its own module, not on the trait that
# re-exports the type. Two cases pointed at the re-export from the day this
# file was written and matched nothing; `mutation-cases` says so, and said so
# to nobody for a week because the lane it runs in was already red on an
# earlier target. Both are re-aimed here.
mutation_targets backends/rust/runtime/src/payload_reading.rs
mutation_targets tools/codegen/templates/rust/process_transition.rs.jinja2
mutation_targets backends/rust/tests/src/integration/undecodable_payload_is_reported/undecodable_payload_is_reported_sm.rs
mutation_regen scripts/regen_undecodable_payload_is_reported.sh

mutation_oracles backends/rust/tests/tests/undecodable_payload_is_reported.rs

# ── The count itself ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The engine goes back to knowing and not saying. Every driver's first
# assertion is this one.

mutation_case "the engine stops recording the reading it was told" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """        if reading.is_undecodable() {
            self.undecodable_payloads = self.undecodable_payloads.saturating_add(1);
            self.last_undecodable_payload = Some(event);
        }""",
     """        if false {
            self.undecodable_payloads = self.undecodable_payloads.saturating_add(1);
            self.last_undecodable_payload = Some(event);
        }""")
PY

# ── An always-true count ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A count that fires for every delivery is exactly as useless as one that never
# fires, and only the prose and intact-payload assertions catch it. W3C test 562
# sends prose and requires it to arrive as a string: a diagnostic that fires
# when nothing is wrong is one nobody reads.

mutation_case "every delivery with a payload is counted" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """        if reading.is_undecodable() {""",
     """        if reading != PayloadReading::Absent {""")
PY

# ── The generated call site drops the value again ──────────────────
#
# The failure shape this whole axis came out of: the reading is computed
# correctly and then discarded at the binding, which no compiler objects to.
# The bridge carries the mutated TEMPLATE into the committed tree.

mutation_case "the generated binding drops the reading" <<'PY'
edit("tools/codegen/templates/rust/process_transition.rs.jinja2",
     """        engine.note_payload_reading(event, payload_reading);""",
     """        let _ = payload_reading;""")
PY

# ── The rung the clause splits in two ──────────────────────────────
#
# `of_text` is the one rule that separates prose from content that asked for
# structure. Answering `Text` for everything is the pre-fixture state with the
# plumbing intact — the count exists, is wired, and never fires.

mutation_case "content that announces an object is read as prose" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/payload_reading.rs",
     """        match payload.trim_start().as_bytes().first() {
            Some(b'{' | b'[') => Self::Undecodable,
            _ => Self::Text,
        }""",
     """        match payload.trim_start().as_bytes().first() {
            Some(b'{' | b'[') => Self::Text,
            _ => Self::Text,
        }""")
PY

# ── The other side of the same rule ────────────────────────────────
#
# Prose reported as a lost payload. The clause's third reading is the NORMAL
# outcome for a `<content>` element an author wrote, so this is the mutation
# that turns the statistic into noise rather than into silence.

mutation_case "prose is reported as a payload that failed to parse" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/payload_reading.rs",
     """        match payload.trim_start().as_bytes().first() {
            Some(b'{' | b'[') => Self::Undecodable,
            _ => Self::Text,
        }""",
     """        match payload.trim_start().as_bytes().first() {
            Some(b'{' | b'[') => Self::Undecodable,
            _ => Self::Undecodable,
        }""")
PY

# ── The successful parse reports the wrong rung ────────────────────
#
# One rung up: the JSON parsed, and the engine says it did not. The fixture's
# fourth delivery is what refuses this — it reaches `accepted`, so the
# structured read demonstrably happened.

mutation_case "a payload that parsed is reported as one that did not" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/lua/src/lib.rs",
     """                    reading = PayloadReading::Structured;""",
     """                    reading = PayloadReading::of_text(event_data);""")
PY

# ── Naming the delivery ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The count says a payload was lost; a host debugging a stalled supervisor
# needs to know which delivery lost it. Leaving it at `None` still counts
# correctly.

mutation_case "the lost payload is counted but its delivery never named" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """            self.last_undecodable_payload = Some(event);""",
     """            self.last_undecodable_payload = None;""")
PY

# ── Naming the wrong delivery ──────────────────────────────────────
#
# The record has to track the last event THAT LOST A PAYLOAD, not the last
# event. Only the fixture's two-event sequence separates the two, which is why
# the second loss arrives under a different name.

mutation_case "every delivery overwrites the name of the last loss" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """        if reading.is_undecodable() {
            self.undecodable_payloads = self.undecodable_payloads.saturating_add(1);
            self.last_undecodable_payload = Some(event);
        }""",
     """        self.last_undecodable_payload = Some(event);
        if reading.is_undecodable() {
            self.undecodable_payloads = self.undecodable_payloads.saturating_add(1);
        }""")
PY
